How Request Flows in Kubernetes

Example flow:
User runs deployment YAML
Request goes to API server
API server stores data in etcd
Scheduler selects worker node
Kubelet creates pod on node
Container runtime starts container
kube-proxy enables networking



“Kubernetes has a control-plane and worker-node architecture.

The Control Plane is responsible for managing the overall cluster state. The main components are kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, and 
kube-controller-manager.

The kube-apiserver is the central entry point for Kubernetes API requests. When I execute a kubectl command, the request reaches the API Server, 
where authentication, authorization, validation, and admission processing take place.

etcd is the distributed key-value store where Kubernetes maintains the cluster state and configuration.

The scheduler is responsible for selecting an appropriate worker node for newly created Pods based on resource requirements, affinity, 
taints and tolerations, and other scheduling constraints.

The controller manager continuously compares the desired state with the actual state and takes corrective action. For example, 
if a Deployment requires three replicas but only two Pods are running, the controllers work to create the missing Pod.

Worker nodes are responsible for running application workloads. Each node has kubelet, a container runtime such as containerd, and 
networking components such as kube-proxy depending on the cluster networking implementation.

The kubelet communicates with the API Server and ensures that the Pods assigned to its node are running correctly. 
The container runtime is responsible for creating and running the containers.

Pods are the smallest deployable units in Kubernetes. Applications are usually exposed internally through Services, 
which provide a stable endpoint even though Pod IPs can change.

For external HTTP/HTTPS traffic, we can use Ingress together with an ingress controller. In AWS EKS, for example, 
the AWS Load Balancer Controller can provision and configure an AWS Application Load Balancer.

From an application perspective, the traffic can flow as DNS → Load Balancer → Ingress → Service → Pod.

So, in simple terms, the Control Plane manages the desired state and scheduling, while Worker Nodes run the actual application workloads.”

